    "body": "Freeport's Grasberg operation. Credit: Freeport McMoRan via YouTube.\nBy: Northern Miner Staff November 16, 2026\nA push by Indonesia to capture at least 70% of the benefits from mining permits sent local mining shares lower Monday, as President Prabowo Subianto raised the prospect of greater state ownership without explaining how the proposed share would be calculated. Prabowo instructed ministers to increase the state’s share of mining returns during a speech Saturday in Palu, Central Sulawesi, news agencies including Reuters and Bloomberg reported. His office described the target as a share of proceeds from managing mining permits, leaving the treatment of existing operations unspecified. “From now on, all mining permits, at least 70% must benefit the state,” Prabowo said in Indonesian. “If necessary, the shares should be state-controlled.” The intervention could affect investment decisions in the world’s dominant nickel producer and a major copper supplier. Indonesia mined an estimated 2.6 million tonnes of nickel last year, about two-thirds of global output, according to the U.S. Geological Survey. Amman Mineral Internasional (IDX: AMMN) shares dropped 3.5% and Merdeka Copper Gold (IDX: MDKA) fell 4.8% in Monday trading. That compared with a 1.1% decline in the Jakarta Composite Index, Dow Jones reported.\nNo timetable\nThe remarks marked an escalation in Indonesia’s rhetoric on state control, BMO Capital Markets commodities analysts Helen Amos and George Heppel said in a Monday note.\nThe presidency’s account didn’t define whether the 70% would be measured against revenue, profit or another measure of economic benefit. It also gave no implementation timetable. The statement therefore leaves investors without a formula for assessing the financial impact on individual mines. A separate proposal earlier this year envisaged allocating 70% of mineral and coal output to the government and 30% to operators under a gross-split arrangement. Energy Minister Bahlil Lahadalia said in June that the plan had been dropped after consultation with Prabowo, BMO noted. Prabowo also called for exports to pass through a designated agency to prevent companies understating shipment volumes. His office presented the measure as a way to improve oversight of natural-resource earnings.\nExisting stakes\nIndonesia already holds a 51% interest in PT Freeport Indonesia, the operator of the Grasberg copper-gold complex. Freeport-McMoRan (NYSE: FCX) owns the remaining 49%. The government has separately outlined plans to raise its Freeport Indonesia stake to 63% by 2041 in connection with extending the operation’s contract. That timetable, described by Lahadalia in February, illustrates how changes in state ownership can be tied to specific operating agreements. Saturday’s broader demand leaves a different question for miners: how the government would count taxes, royalties, dividends and ownership towards its proposed share, and whether existing agreements would be protected.\nPrint\nTAG: copper production Indonesia Nickel regulations\nCOMMODITY: Copper Iron Ore Nickel\nREGION: Asia\nCOMPANY: Freeport McMoRan\nRepublish this article",
